9.theory:
## scanf return

`scanf` returns the **number of successfully read arguments**:

```c
int n;
int result = scanf("%d", &n);
if (result == 1) {
    printf("Read: %d\n", n);
} else {
    printf("Input error\n");
}
```

- `1` — read one value
- `0` — failed to read (not a number)
- `EOF` — end of file

## Problem with errors

If the user enters `abc` instead of a number, `scanf` **does not advance** the pointer, keeps reading the same thing. Infinite loop:

```c
while (scanf("%d", &n) != 1) {
    // will loop — abc doesn't advance
}
```

Solution — flush the buffer (read a line and parse):

```c
char buf[100];
while (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin)) {
    if (sscanf(buf, "%d", &n) == 1) break;
    printf("Enter a number: ");
}
```

## Simple input

For most educational tasks, **sufficient** `scanf("%d", &n)` — assumes input is correct.

## sscanf

`sscanf` — reads **from a string**, not stdin:

```c
int a, b;
sscanf("10 20", "%d %d", &a, &b);   // a=10, b=20
```

Handy for parsing strings.

## What if

- Don't check return — input error → garbage.
- `%d` but entered `abc` — sticking.

## Common mistake

`while (scanf(...) != 1)` without flushing buffer — infinite loop.

## Summary

- `scanf` returns number of items read
- `1` — success, `0` — wrong type, `EOF` — end
- `sscanf` — from a string
- Check the return for reliability

