11.theory:
## Classic switch

```c
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        printf("Mon\n");
        break;
    case 2:
        printf("Tue\n");
        break;
    default:
        printf("Other\n");
}
```

- `switch (expression)` — only integer (`int`, `char`, `enum`)
- `break` — exit switch
- `default` — if no case matched

## Fall-through

Without `break`, execution **falls through** to the next `case`:

```c
switch (x) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
        printf("1 or 2\n");
        break;
}
```

Empty `case`s group together. Sometimes used intentionally:

```c
switch (grade) {
    case 'A':
    case 'B':
        printf("Good\n");
        break;
    case 'C':
        printf("Average\n");
        break;
}
```

## switch vs if

`switch` — exact match with several values. `if` — ranges, complex conditions.

## What if

- Forget `break` — falls into following cases.
- `switch` with `double` — compile error.
- `switch` with `float` — not allowed.
- Duplicate `case` — error.

## Common mistake

Forget `break` — program executes extra `case`s.

## Summary

- `switch (int/char)` — exact match
- `break` — exit, otherwise fall-through
- `default` — fallback
- Empty `case` — grouping
- Integers only

