14.theory:
## while

```c
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
}
```

Check **before** body. If the condition is immediately false — body doesn't run once.

## do-while

```c
int i = 1;
do {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
} while (i <= 5);
```

Check **after** body. Runs **at least once**. `;` after `while (...)`.

## When while

- Don't know iterations in advance.
- Read input until "exit".
- Wait for an event.

## When do-while

- Body must run at least once.
- Program menu.

## Example: sum until 0

```c
int total = 0;
int n;
while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1 && n != 0) {
    total += n;
}
printf("Sum: %d\n", total);
```

## Infinite

```c
while (1) {
    // exit via break
}
```

## What if

- Forget `i++` — infinite.
- `do-while` without `;` — compile error.
- `while (1)` without break — forever.

## Common mistake

Forget `;` after `while (...)` in `do-while`.

## Summary

- `while (cond)` — check before
- `do { } while (cond);` — after, `;` mandatory
- `do-while` — at least once
- `while (1)` + `break` — standard

