15.theory:
## break — exit

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;
    printf("%d\n", i);   // 0, 1, 2
}
```

## continue — skip iteration

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;
    printf("%d\n", i);   // 0, 1, 3, 4
}
```

## Difference

| `break` | `continue` |
|---|---|
| Exits completely | Skips one iteration |

## In while

`break` and `continue` work the same.

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 10) {
    i++;
    if (i == 5) break;
    printf("%d\n", i);
}
```

Note: `i++` before `continue`, otherwise infinite.

## break in nested loops

`break` exits only the **current** loop.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (j == 1) break;
        printf("%d,%d\n", i, j);
    }
}
```

## What if

- `continue` before `i++` — infinite loop.
- `break` outside loop — compile error.

## Common mistake

In `while` with `continue` forgot to update the counter — infinite loop.

## Summary

- `break` — exit
- `continue` — skip iteration
- `break` in nested — current only
- `continue` in while — update counter before it

