20.theory:
## Via for with index

```c
int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};
int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", arr[i]);
}
```

## Sum

```c
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    sum += arr[i];
}
printf("%d\n", sum);
```

## Maximum

```c
int max = arr[0];
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];
}
```

Start from `arr[0]`, not `0` — if all negative, max would be `0`.

## Search

```c
int target = 20;
int found = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (arr[i] == target) {
        found = 1;
        break;
    }
}
```

## for-each in C?

C has **no** for-each for arrays. Only `for` with index. In C++ — range-based for.

## 2D arrays

```c
int matrix[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        printf("%d ", matrix[i][j]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}
```

## What if

- `i <= n` instead of `< n` — out of bounds.
- Max from `0` — wrong for negatives.

## Common mistake

`for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)` — one extra iteration, UB.

## Summary

- `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` — standard
- `sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0])` — length
- Accumulator — before loop
- Max — from `arr[0]`
- `<=` — mistake

