27.theory:
## struct

`struct` — custom composite type: a set of fields.

```c
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

struct Point p;
p.x = 10;
p.y = 20;
```

## typedef

Type alias:

```c
typedef struct {
    int x;
    int y;
} Point;

Point p;
p.x = 10;
```

Without `typedef` you'd write `struct Point`. With it — just `Point`.

## Initialization

```c
Point p = {10, 20};
Point q = {.x = 10, .y = 20};   // designated initializers (C99)
```

## Nested

```c
typedef struct {
    Point top_left;
    Point bottom_right;
} Rect;

Rect r = {{0, 0}, {10, 10}};
```

## Array of structs

```c
Point points[3] = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}};
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    printf("%d,%d\n", points[i].x, points[i].y);
}
```

## Strings in structs

```c
typedef struct {
    char name[20];
    int age;
} Person;

Person p;
strcpy(p.name, "Ivan");    // copy
p.age = 25;
```

**Cannot** `p.name = "Ivan"` — array not reassignable.

## What if

- `p.name = "Ivan"` — error.
- Forget `;` after `struct { }` — error.
- Use `struct Point` without typedef — write `struct` everywhere.

## Common mistake

`p.name = "Ivan"` for `char name[20]` — error. Need `strcpy`.

## Summary

- `struct` — composite type
- `typedef` — alias
- `.` — field access
- `strcpy` for strings
- `{.x = 10}` — designated
- `;` after `struct { }`

