28.theory:
## Pointer to struct

```c
typedef struct {
    int x, y;
} Point;

Point p = {3, 7};
Point *ptr = &p;
```

## Access via `->`

```c
printf("%d\n", ptr->x);   // 3
printf("%d\n", ptr->y);   // 7
```

`ptr->x` = `(*ptr).x`. `->` is more convenient.

You can also use `(*ptr).x`, but `->` is the standard.

## Change through pointer

```c
ptr->x = 100;
printf("%d\n", p.x);   // 100
```

## Passing to a function

Large structs are **more efficiently** passed by pointer:

```c
void move(Point *p, int dx, int dy) {
    p->x += dx;
    p->y += dy;
}

Point p = {0, 0};
move(&p, 5, 10);
printf("%d,%d\n", p.x, p.y);   // 5,10
```

Without pointer — copy, changes not returned.

## Array of structs

```c
Point arr[3] = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}};
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    printf("%d,%d\n", arr[i].x, arr[i].y);
}
```

`arr[i]` — the struct itself, `arr[i].x` — field.

## Pointer to array of structs

```c
Point *ptr = arr;
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", ptr[i].x);   // or (ptr + i)->x
}
```

## const

```c
void printPoint(const Point *p) {
    printf("%d\n", p->x);   // read only
    // p->x = 5; // error
}
```

## What if

- `ptr.x` instead of `ptr->x` — compile error.
- Forget `&` when passing — copy.
- Modify `const` struct — error.

## Common mistake

`ptr.x` for a pointer — error. Only `ptr->x`.

## Summary

- `Point *ptr = &p`
- `ptr->x` = `(*ptr).x`
- `ptr->x = ...` — modify
- In a function — via pointer for modification
- `const` — read only

